   old=r"""Three alternative paradigms were considered and rejected. A qualitative interview study of Norwegian implementing actors would have produced descriptive knowledge about how practitioners currently perceive the retention conflict, but would not have produced a mechanism, and would have required ethical clearance and participant access disproportionate to the contribution. A systematic literature review would have consolidated the state of knowledge, a function partly discharged within Chapter 2, but the review itself established that the gap is the absence of a design, not the absence of a synthesis. A purely formal-methods study in the tradition of Feng, Marsso, Sabetzadeh, and Chechik (2023) would have produced the model without the artefact, and would have required proof-assistant machinery and expertise outside the scope of this programme. DSR, in the variant developed in Section 3.3, permits the model and the artefact to be produced together, with the model supplying the rigour and the artefact supplying the relevance.""",
